WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.157
- You know most firms would put you

00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:04.060
in front of the bar and
have your license stripped.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.520
You lied to a client
and he figured it out.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.180
- Yes, exactly!

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.620
Don't lie to a client.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.838
You should not lie to a
client and defraud them.

00:00:12.838 --> 00:00:15.421
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.780
Hey legal eagles, D. James Stone here

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.480
teaching you how to think like a lawyer.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.380
Today we're tackling a fan favorite,

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.570
highly requested, Suits.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:29.000
Now I've never seen a full
episode of Suits before

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.270
but I'm guessing from the title

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.960
that it's about a group
of men who wear suits.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.330
You can tell I'm very
perceptive about these things.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:41.250
As always if you see any
legal or factual inaccuracies

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.600
please leave me a
comment below in the form

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:44.660
of an objection.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.870
If you object I will get
to it and I will either

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.820
sustain or overrule your objection.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:50.700
But let's dig right in,

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.270
oh and by the way stick around

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.850
until the end of the
episode where I give suits

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.810
episode one a grade for legal realism

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.160
so you won't wanna miss that.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.880
But without further ado let's dig in

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.090
to Suits, episode one.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.080
- First of all Gerald

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.710
if you think anyone's
gonna touch this deal

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.593
after your bad faith you're mistaken.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.210
Second the way our agreement works

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.810
is the minute Cooper signed the deal

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.390
which gave you everything you wanted

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.000
our fee was due and payable

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.140
which is why at 7:30 I
received confirmation

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.750
of a wire transfer from escrow

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.573
indicating payment in full.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.940
- Wait, is the deal done or not?

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.870
No transaction on the planet would have

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.370
someone transfer a huge amount of money

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.090
before the deal is consummated.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.870
Now, there are companies that
deal in this sort of thing

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.310
they're called escrow companies.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:45.010
If there is an amount that
is going from one company

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.460
to another then you give your money

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.360
to an escrow company.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.080
They hold it until all the
deal terms are complete

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.520
and then they turn it over
when certain criteria are met.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.340
If any lawyer's drafted a
deal that had a wire transfer

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.550
going before the terms are complete

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.050
that's probably malpractice.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.890
So this is crazy.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.410
I don't know what deal
they're talking about

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.760
but that would not happen.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.730
- We got paid before
Gerald signed the deal?

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:12.600
- What are you talking about?

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.680
This is a memo about some
fire drill on Tuesday.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:16.739
You're the blue team captain.

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:17.809
You get to wear a fire hat.

00:02:17.809 --> 00:02:22.050
- Okay, alright so what
this person has done

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.100
is called fraud.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.120
He has made up facts that are not true

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.550
in order to induce someone
to enter into an agreement.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.120
When you induce someone based on fraud

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.260
that is called fraud in the inducement,

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.740
and when you commit
fraud in the inducement

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.430
that deal is null and void
because you fraudulently

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.990
got them to enter into a deal.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.900
So, if this deal goes through
and the other guy finds out

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.190
that he's been lied to
in a fraudulent manner

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.220
that deal is no longer done

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.430
and it would be unwound
and the whole thing

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:55.330
would just blow up.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:57.100
So that's not good lawyering.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.210
Good lawyers don't lie,

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:02.620
good lawyers use the facts as
they exist in the real world

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.150
to make sure that all the
parties get their best deal.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.520
They do not lie in order
to consummate a deal.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.430
That is bad lawyering, that is malpractice

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:13.643
and it might be a crime.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:15.630
- [Teacher] I got it.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.663
- Alright, so he's an LSAT cheater.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.490
And he hid his test so
that no one would know

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.372
that he cheated.

00:03:28.372 --> 00:03:29.713
- Hey.

00:03:29.713 --> 00:03:30.849
Hey stop!

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:31.682
You in the cap.

00:03:31.682 --> 00:03:34.349
(intense music)

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:36.700
- This is really interesting.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.220
LSATs or the Law School Entrance Exam

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.040
is very important.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.060
Its how you get into a good law school.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.450
It's how you get into any law school.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.680
So it's not surprising then that given

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.610
how important the LSAT is

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:50.840
there is some cheating.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.620
Now the board that offers the LSAT

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.070
has tried to combat cheating

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.570
so they require you to
bring in a drivers license

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:00.290
or other valid form of
ID and they check that

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.330
when you enter and when you leave.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:04.590
But sometimes people get around that.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.040
Given how important it
is it's not surprising

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.300
that there are some really
nefarious people out there

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.110
who are going to try to game the system

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:12.830
and try and get a good LSAT score

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:17.177
because it can change the
course of your entire career.

00:04:17.177 --> 00:04:18.290
(intense music)

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:19.717
- [Man] What did you get me?

00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:21.940
- What I said I was gonna get you, a 158.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.600
- I told you I wanted a 175.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:24.500
- I told you only one

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.610
out of a hundred people can score that.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.460
- That's right if you score a 175

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.220
that means you're in the 99th percentile.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.050
Actually you're well
into the 99th percentile

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:34.883
of the LSAT.

00:04:34.883 --> 00:04:38.110
It's scored from 120 points to 180 points.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.540
So if you score a 175 that means that you

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.080
are basically in the 99.5 percentile.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.520
That is very, very high and
so he's not exaggerating

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.180
when he says a 175 is only procured

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.770
by one out of a hundred people.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:55.560
And he said he scored a 158.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.280
That's not a great score, that's probably

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.760
in the 80th or 85th percentile

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.830
so not bad, but certainly not a score

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.370
that's going to get you
into one of the top tier

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:08.580
law schools out there.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.920
So it's funny that he's
complaining about the score

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:14.480
that he cheated and got from this guy.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.940
- We need people who think on their feet,

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:17.730
not another clone with a rod up his ass.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.420
- Harvey the fact that
we only hire from Harvard

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.780
gives us a cache that's
a little more valuable

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.373
than hiring a kid from Rutgers.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.143
- You went to Harvard Law.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:28.840
- I'm an exception.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:29.940
- Find me another one.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.080
- Alright so the recruiting process

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.350
is a fascinating process in law school

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:38.110
and with firms that I
could talk about for hours.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:41.980
Suffice it to say there
are firms that only hire

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:42.813
from certain schools.

00:05:42.813 --> 00:05:45.020
Now there probably aren't any law firms

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:46.860
that hire only from Harvard.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.180
You're probably gonna
hire from the top five

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.750
or the top 10 or the top 20 law schools,

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.830
but there are law firms
that are very picky

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:55.980
about where they get their
summer associates from.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:59.340
Now a summer associate is
a law firm specific term.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.560
It means someone that's in law school,

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.930
usually this is someone

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:04.600
who is in their second year of law school,

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.520
their second year summer

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.470
and they're basically
interning for the firm.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:12.110
And if you like that person
at the end of the summer

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:15.470
then you'll give them an offer
to come work for the firm

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.030
when they graduate from law school.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:18.890
That's what they're
saying when they're saying

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.650
they are looking for a summer associate.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:23.220
That's a very common thing
for big firms to look for.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.320
So it's cool that they
get that lingo down.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.893
That's absolutely accurate.

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:30.903
- Great thanks.

00:06:30.903 --> 00:06:31.736
- Okay.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.440
- Dot, we're gonna need
to streamline this.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.993
Give each guy a hard time
before you send them back,

00:06:37.993 --> 00:06:40.650
keep me awake if they say
something clever, cool?

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:41.935
- Okay.

00:06:41.935 --> 00:06:43.920
What are you looking for?

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:44.753
- Another me.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:47.730
- Okay this is great.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.640
He's interviewing the
law students at a hotel

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.500
which is exactly where law
students tend to get interviewed

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.060
for these, what are called
on campus interviews.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.890
Now I went through the
process in Los Angeles.

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.640
I assume the process is
pretty similar in New York.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.680
The law students all enter
their names into a lottery

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.930
then you send everyone to a hotel.

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:11.510
You get a bunch of
associates or junior partners

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:14.830
in the law firm to hang
out in a hotel room.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.770
The law students come in and
they conduct their interview.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.900
So, so far that aspect
of the recruiting process

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:22.810
in law school is spot on.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.900
It's so funny, I've never
seen that in a TV show before.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:30.650
- So Chip, what makes you
think that I'm gonna let

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.120
the whitest man that I have ever seen

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.543
interview for our firm?

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.638
- Because I have an appointment.

00:07:36.638 --> 00:07:39.221
(upbeat music)

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.090
- And lawyers have all kinds of criteria

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:48.280
as to how they choose
people for callbacks.

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:49.530
These are screening interviews.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.870
They're just going through
dozens, if not hundreds

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:52.930
of law students

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.870
and if they like you then
they send you for a callback

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:57.930
where you'll be interviewed
by a bunch of attorneys

00:07:57.930 --> 00:07:59.210
at the law firm itself.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.310
So you're really churning
and burning in the hotel

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.730
and it's funny that
this guy has a criteria

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:05.483
of they have to say a witty remark.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:09.490
- If you want this job so much

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.290
why don't you just go to law school?

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.890
- When I was in college it
was my dream to be a lawyer.

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:19.120
I needed some money
and Trevor convinced me

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.023
to memorize this math test and sell it.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.720
Turns out we sold it
to the Dean's daughter.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.420
I lost my scholarship, I got
kicked out of school I...

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.603
I got knocked into a different life.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:39.860
- There's some truth to that.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:42.380
In law school, and
especially when you're trying

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.120
to become a member of the bar

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:46.400
they look at your moral character

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.490
and if you have a blemish on it

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:49.810
you're going to have to explain it.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:54.810
Now, a youthful indiscretion
with no follow up conduct

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:56.990
and no criminal record,
I don't think that's

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:59.930
gonna be a huge barrier
to becoming an attorney.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:01.950
You will have to explain it to the board

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:03.930
but it shouldn't preclude you

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:07.320
from entering into the
profession for your entire life.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:08.680
You just have to explain it.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:09.670
Explain that you're not going

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:11.290
to do something like that again.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:13.083
- I need a grown goddamn man.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:17.320
- You give me this and I
will work as hard as it takes

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:18.617
to school those Harvard douches

00:09:18.617 --> 00:09:21.013
and become the best
lawyer you have ever seen.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:23.160
- I'm inclined to give you a shot

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.000
but what if I decide to go another way?

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.000
- I say that's fair,

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.660
but sometimes I like
to hang out with people

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:29.823
who aren't that bright, you know,

00:09:29.823 --> 00:09:31.783
just to see how the other half lives.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:35.040
- Move over, I'm emailing the firm

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.793
we just found our next associate.

00:09:36.793 --> 00:09:39.376
(upbeat music)

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.100
- The problem here is that
he has ethical limitations

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:48.930
as well and if he is
knowingly sort of conspiring

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:51.370
to have someone who's
not a member of the bar

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:53.880
participate in these activities

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.490
that is a huge problem both for Mike,

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.560
but also for Harvey because he's the one

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:01.410
that's sort of knowingly supporting it

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.740
so he may run into ethical issues himself

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:08.130
that might result in him
losing his bar license

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:11.340
by knowingly hiring someone
that isn't an attorney

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:12.610
to do attorney's work.

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:15.810
So big problems there for both of them.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:17.730
- Gerald Tate fired the firm.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:19.580
- So you're taking my promotion away?

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.580
- You know most firms would
put you in front of the bar

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:23.850
and have your license stripped.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:26.290
You lied to a client
and he figured it out.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:27.960
- Yes, exactly!

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.410
Don't lie to a client.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:33.010
You should not lie to a
client and defraud them.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:36.580
And, oh my God, he's a
client, not the opposite side.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:39.600
Why would you even lie to your client?

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.270
You're not even trying to close
a deal from the other side.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:44.760
I mean you have an ethical obligation

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:48.060
to do what your client wants

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.170
or to get the outcome
that your client wants.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:53.320
To lie and defraud your
own personal client?

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.080
He should be disbarred for that.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:58.450
That's crazy, don't do that.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:00.510
Pro tip, don't lie to your client.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:01.920
- No more shenanigans.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.800
You do one more thing that
isn't straight as an arrow

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.260
and instead of covering your ass

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:09.260
I'll put you in front of the bar myself.

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:14.490
- Mike.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:17.000
- Hey, who's ready for a great first day?

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.280
- I'm gonna have to let you go.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:20.530
- That's probably a good
thing to do at this point.

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:22.400
- For lying to a client
and if they find out

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:23.940
that I lied about you
going to Harvard they'll--

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:26.670
- Oh, and by the way, the
whole partnership model

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:28.520
of a law firm means that the partners

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:30.260
have joint and several liability.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:33.730
So if one of the partners
conducts some horrible fraud

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:36.510
on their client, the whole
partnership is at risk

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:37.343
at that point.

00:11:37.343 --> 00:11:40.110
So, you know, you gotta be really careful

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.330
and you have to worry about
what your other partners

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.550
are doing, so to cover up for a partner

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:47.970
who is defrauding and
ignoring their own client

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:51.040
to bully them into a
deal, that is not good.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.763
This firm is not going to
exist for very much longer.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.693
- I'll um, I'll give you your promotion,

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.300
but you have to do something for me.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:02.830
- [Harvey] Anything.

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:04.290
- Just digging it deeper.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.123
- Pro bono.

00:12:05.123 --> 00:12:05.970
- Anything but that.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.480
- Harvey, pro bono cases
are how we as a firm

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:10.910
show that we care about
more than just ourselves.

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.130
- I'm not saying we shouldn't do them,

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:14.400
I'm saying I shouldn't do them.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:15.233
- And it's how you--

00:12:15.233 --> 00:12:17.560
- A lot of lawyers feel that
way about pro bono work.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:19.560
You generally have an ethical obligation

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:22.350
to do some pro bono work during the year

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:25.460
so you can help those who
can't afford the large fees

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.340
that most law firms command.

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:29.900
But that being said some
lawyers really hate doing it.

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:31.260
Some lawyers really like doing it

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:32.810
because you can get experience

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.900
that you wouldn't otherwise
be able to procure

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:37.430
like going to trial more often than not

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:40.030
or working one on one with a client.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.410
When you're a junior
associate you often don't get

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:44.530
those opportunities.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:47.430
But if you're a senior
partner you can understand

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:48.990
why they don't wanna take that away

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.120
from their billable practice.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:52.990
Still, kind of a dick move though.

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:55.100
- One night he says
that if I sleep with him

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:56.880
he's gonna take care of me in the firm

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.480
and if I don't he's gonna fire me.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:02.550
- So what did you do?
- I went to human resources.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:03.810
They said they investigated,

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:06.130
couldn't find anything
to support my claim.

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:07.820
I wasn't looking for a payoff.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:10.480
All I wanted was for it to stop.

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:11.433
Can you help me?

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:14.213
- Uh, yes.

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:16.417
Yes, I can.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:19.880
- So generally speaking
there are two types

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:20.850
of sexual harassment.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.970
There is the hostile work
environment sexual harassment

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:27.846
where by the actions of someone it creates

00:13:27.846 --> 00:13:30.860
an environment that is
non-conducive to working

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:32.520
that makes people feel uncomfortable

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:34.270
such that they can't work there,

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.730
and you know, that's
generally referred to as

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:38.070
creating a hostile work environment.

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:39.290
You're not allowed to do that.

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:41.580
But the second type of sexual harassment

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:45.570
that is really less common
because it's so much worse

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:49.230
and so much more explicit is
quid pro quo sexual harassment

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:51.150
which is what this person describes

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:55.660
where you are conditioning
a job or a promotion

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:59.590
or some perk on some sexual favor

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:01.080
and it's a really terrible thing.

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:02.850
It definitely happens.

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:05.800
Thankfully it's more
rare than it used to be.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:08.090
So if that client is to be believed

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:11.890
that is a classic case of quid
pro quo sexual harassment.

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.440
Totally illegal, but it's
very hard for victims

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:19.440
of that kind of harassment
to prove what has happened

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:22.023
because it's a classic
case of he said, she said.

00:14:23.146 --> 00:14:24.913
- I don't know how to fill out a subpoena.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:28.193
- Come on genius.

00:14:28.193 --> 00:14:29.850
How hard is it to fill out a form?

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:32.250
- Donna, can you show me
how to fill out a subpoena?

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:33.410
- Absolutely, and after that

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:34.930
you want me to show you
how to wipe your ass?

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:36.120
- It's funny because
you should already know

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:37.667
how to do both those things.

00:14:37.667 --> 00:14:38.500
- No, no.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.430
No summer associate
would know how to do that

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:42.263
just right off the bat.

00:14:42.263 --> 00:14:43.950
Now true, he could look at the form

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:46.140
and he could probably
muddle his way through

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.380
but the funny thing about
that is if he files a subpoena

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:52.010
with the court, or sends a
subpoena to the other side

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:56.550
and signs it, he has effectively
pretended to be an attorney

00:14:56.550 --> 00:14:59.220
and engaged in the
unauthorized practice of law

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:01.960
which would get basically
all of them disbarred,

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:03.290
especially him.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:07.280
So everyone there at
that firm has obligation

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:08.820
to make sure that he does it right

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:10.598
and they shouldn't be chastising him

00:15:10.598 --> 00:15:12.880
for not knowing how to
fill out a particular form

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:16.290
and in fact for any summer
associate if they fill out

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:18.730
a subpoena or discovery request

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:21.000
then an attorney's gonna have to review it

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:24.810
and sign it and if you sign
that you are basically saying

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:27.360
that it's as good as
if you did it yourself

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:30.490
and it is accurate and
it complies with the law

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:33.430
and you can be sanctioned
as if you wrote it yourself.

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:35.320
You cannot allow a summer associate

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:39.012
to take credit or practice law.

00:15:39.012 --> 00:15:41.850
So all their asses are on the line here.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:44.814
They should not be
chastising him for this act

00:15:44.814 --> 00:15:46.560
of filling out a subpoena.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.970
- Okay, I have two words for you.

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:51.400
Absolutely beautiful.

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.050
- You think you're the only
one who can charm a client?

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:56.460
- Categorically stunning.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.160
- So what most people don't
know about being a big firm

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:03.260
attorney is that without
clients the firm can't operate.

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:06.080
So generally there are a couple
different types of partners.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.240
One are the partners that
just bill like crazy.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:11.420
They're often the best attorneys.

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:13.990
They're the ones that
really put in the hours

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:16.440
and they're bringing
revenue into the firm.

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:18.050
Now there's another type of partner

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:21.540
which are the rainmakers,
the people that more focus

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.530
on bringing clients in.

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:25.860
Now both of those types
of partners are important

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:29.430
because without clients
there's no work to be done.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:34.430
So often an attorney's job
is not just the nine to five

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:38.380
court pleadings and
dealing with transactions.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:40.270
There's a lot of client management

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:42.440
where you have to make sure

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:44.250
that there are more clients coming in

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.260
so that you have work to do in the future.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:47.970
- They filed a motion to dismiss the case

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:49.330
based on our lack of evidence.

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:50.163
- Good.

00:16:50.163 --> 00:16:52.780
- Good, what do you mean good?

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:54.010
- They don't wanna hand over the files.

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:54.843
You pressed where it hurt,

00:16:54.843 --> 00:16:56.160
you're looking in the right place.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:57.450
Did you think they wouldn't fight back?

00:16:57.450 --> 00:16:59.089
- Well, I...

00:16:59.089 --> 00:16:59.922
Now that you mention it.

00:16:59.922 --> 00:17:00.755
- Oh.

00:17:00.755 --> 00:17:02.240
- Alright, so that doesn't
make any sense at all.

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:04.690
A motion to dismiss is
a motion that you file

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:06.350
on the basis of the pleadings.

00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:07.680
So, they're the plaintiff here.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.350
They file what's called a complaint

00:17:09.350 --> 00:17:10.580
which lays out their whole story.

00:17:10.580 --> 00:17:14.270
That she was fired and
the other side is allowed

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:17.320
to either file an answer
to that or file a motion

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:19.290
to dismiss in most jurisdictions.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:20.710
The part that doesn't make any sense here

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:24.110
is that the motion to
dismiss takes the allegations

00:17:24.110 --> 00:17:25.660
and the complaint at face value.

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:27.470
So there's no evidence at this point.

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:30.740
There's been no opportunity for discovery,

00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:32.230
it's also been just a couple of days

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:34.440
so these things take months.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.450
But you can't file a motion to dismiss

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:38.210
on the basis the other side
doesn't have any evidence

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:40.760
because no one has
gathered any evidence yet.

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:43.180
So that doesn't make any sense.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:45.110
- I care about this woman.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:46.053
She's got nowhere else to turn--

00:17:46.053 --> 00:17:48.373
- And you can't help her by yourself.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:53.613
- I can, I just prefer not to.

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:57.620
- This is a really good example of why

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:00.890
you would never hire someone
who did not go to law school

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:03.170
and doesn't have any legal experience.

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:06.610
The law and being a lawyer
is all about application.

00:18:06.610 --> 00:18:09.370
It's not about the memorization of facts.

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:11.550
I can go online and I can look up

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:14.060
what the rule is, what the law is.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:15.540
I can look up the case law.

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:17.610
I don't need to have a photographic memory

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:18.630
to make a good argument.

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:21.450
I will look up the
authority that is relevant.

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:25.200
The idea that this guy would
be a cracker jack associate

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:29.230
simply on the basis of
reading a bunch of law books

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.930
makes no sense and it's
why, realistically,

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.970
he needs to ask for help from
people who have experience

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:36.620
because they're the ones that can help him

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:37.890
because they've been in the trenches

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:38.890
on this kind of stuff.

00:18:38.890 --> 00:18:40.990
So, it's good of him to ask for help.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:43.320
That's what a junior associate should do

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:45.610
but he's not gonna be a good attorney

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:48.250
just on the basis of having a good memory.

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:51.310
- Okay, you're asking
to look at private files

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:52.790
without any evidence of your assertion.

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:54.680
- Yeah, but the only way
that we can find the evidence

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:56.000
is to look at those files.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:56.833
- It's a rickety argument.

00:18:56.833 --> 00:18:57.990
- But is there precedent?

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:03.510
- Oh my God this is such
a stupid discovery battle.

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:06.640
Discovery allows you to get
evidence from the other side

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:09.570
and you don't have to
make a positive showing

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:12.000
in order to get that evidence as long as

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.780
you can demonstrate that the evidence

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:17.040
that you could potentially get is related

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.360
to the allegations that
you've made in your complaint

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:22.050
or are related to
defeating those allegations

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:24.880
or are relevant to your
defense in the case

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:27.260
then you should be able
to get that information.

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:30.050
You know, bounded by
things like privilege.

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:32.000
You can't get things that
are necessarily privileged

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:33.930
or trade secrets or things like that,

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:37.210
but if there was a rule that
said you had to have evidence

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.100
before you can get
evidence well no lawsuits

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:41.070
would ever happen so this is,

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:42.980
this is a stupid argument
that they're dealing with.

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:44.640
This is not the kind of discovery dispute

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:47.040
that you're gonna deal
with as an attorney.

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:49.100
- I'd like to think
Herman would come to me

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:51.463
before it even got to an investigation.

00:19:53.830 --> 00:19:55.870
- I saw that, that should be stricken.

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:57.700
- Well, what if Herman did
come to you, your honor?

00:19:57.700 --> 00:19:58.800
- There's nothing to strike.

00:19:58.800 --> 00:19:59.961
There's nothing on the record.

00:19:59.961 --> 00:20:01.511
(laughs)

00:20:01.511 --> 00:20:02.830
- By firing him under false pretenses.

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:04.670
- You really think that little of me?

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:05.503
- I wouldn't trust you

00:20:05.503 --> 00:20:08.383
as far as I can throw you, your honor.

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:14.720
- Hand over the files today, that's it.

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:18.350
- It's a little ridiculous
to hand over the files today.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.260
Usually you would have several
weeks to go over the files

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:22.620
and then turn them over.

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:25.050
You have to be able to review
them before you turn them over

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:26.070
to the other side.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:27.448
You have to check for
things like relevance.

00:20:27.448 --> 00:20:31.820
You don't just get access
to millions of pages

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:34.290
of documents just by requesting them.

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:36.890
But this whole dispute is kind of insane.

00:20:36.890 --> 00:20:39.740
Every civil case goes through
what's called, discovery.

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:42.020
It's the process by which
you ask the other side

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:45.600
for information and the other
side has to turn that over

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.580
unless they can show that
there's a reason not to.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.250
Sometimes that reason is
because it's privileged,

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:53.810
sometimes that reason is
because it's not related

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:54.643
to the lawsuit.

00:20:54.643 --> 00:20:57.000
And even if they were able
to get that information

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:59.360
it wouldn't relate to the
claims that they're making?

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:02.740
But you always have to
engage in some discovery

00:21:02.740 --> 00:21:04.520
and it seems like this
company is just saying,

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:07.940
you have filed a sexual harassment claim

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:10.750
against our company and
you should not be entitled

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:12.380
to any documents whatsoever.

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:15.110
Well, as you can imagine,
that doesn't fly.

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:18.550
You always need some
documents and the company

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:20.530
that is the defendant
is going to be the one

00:21:20.530 --> 00:21:22.450
who possesses those documents.

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:25.100
This is a dispute that would never happen.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:27.120
- [Rachel] They're trying
to bury you in paperwork.

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:30.487
- [Mike] Well, they picked the wrong guy.

00:21:30.487 --> 00:21:33.260
- If they didn't, I did. Get
it done by the end of the week.

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:35.630
I gotta run and charm a new client.

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:38.260
- Yeah, so this is a tactic
that actually happens.

00:21:38.260 --> 00:21:41.690
You often seen big companies
try to bury the other side

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:43.950
in paperwork, especially
if they're going up

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:46.930
against a small firm
or a solo practitioner

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:48.520
that doesn't have the
infrastructure to be able

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.310
to review all of these documents.

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:52.270
However, any major law firm,

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:55.540
and this appears to be a major
law firm in this TV show,

00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:58.030
has in-house document review systems

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:00.200
and what they allow you to do is to scan

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:03.420
thousands and sometimes
millions of pages of paperwork

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:06.480
and to run word searches on it.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:09.450
Not to mention this kind of tactic

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.500
of trying to bury the
other side in paperwork

00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:15.680
is highly disfavored by
federal and state courts.

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:17.790
And if a party tried to do this to me

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:20.280
and give me a huge dump of paper files

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:22.160
I would turn around and
go straight to the judge

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:23.230
and say, this is ridiculous,

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:25.210
they're trying to drive up our costs

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:28.530
and ask them to give it
to me in electronic form.

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:31.080
Or if I couldn't do that
then demand that the court

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:32.420
give me a court order

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:35.030
ordering them to turn
it over in electronic.

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:38.293
- Two months later I was fired
for having a bad attitude.

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:41.340
- This is ridiculous.

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:42.695
- Wipe that smirk off your face.

00:22:42.695 --> 00:22:43.787
You're not even supposed to be here.

00:22:43.787 --> 00:22:46.290
- You really don't want
that guy in the room

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:47.740
during this deposition.

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:48.900
No good can come from that.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.320
- After working for Devlin Macgregor

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:54.440
you then went on to
waitress at Hooters, right?

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:55.273
- Yes, I did.

00:22:55.273 --> 00:22:58.030
- A place that advertises the sexuality

00:22:58.030 --> 00:22:59.380
of its wait staff, correct?

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:02.520
- You'll have to ask them that.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:03.353
- I'm asking you.

00:23:03.353 --> 00:23:05.470
- And she's not answering.

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:06.350
- Alright, this is good.

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:08.690
This is actually what a
deposition looks like.

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:10.900
You sit in a conference table.

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:12.460
You've got attorneys on either side.

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:14.030
Sometimes you have a party representative

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:16.240
and you have a stenographer
who's taking down

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.670
everything that's said
for the official record.

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:20.640
So this is a fairly
accurate representation

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:23.720
of what a big firm deposition looks like.

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:26.340
- I'm sorry, you were
arrested in your past

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:27.900
and you lied about it here under oath.

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:31.083
Is that making it seem
different than what it is?

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:36.863
No answer, alright.

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:40.010
- At this point Harvey and Mike

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:41.480
should be objecting like hell,

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:43.390
they should be instructing
the witness not to answer

00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:46.040
and they should be
chastising the other attorney

00:23:46.040 --> 00:23:47.960
for bringing up this subject

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:51.330
which was a criminal
matter when she was a minor

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:52.710
but also was under seal.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:54.730
How did this guy even
get that information?

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:56.210
I don't know the rule with respect

00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:58.550
to whether or not she can be found liable

00:23:58.550 --> 00:24:01.050
for perjury in this particular context

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:04.040
when the subject was sort
of off limits to begin with

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:05.640
but all of this is wrong.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:06.740
They should be up in arms,

00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:09.370
they should be shutting
this deposition down.

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:11.920
None of this should be on
the record at this point.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:16.190
- You can save your breath.

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:18.823
There's no way I'm testifying
at the hearing tomorrow.

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:22.080
- That's not why I'm here.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.783
I know what you did.
- What are you talking about?

00:24:23.783 --> 00:24:26.087
- You never worked for Devlin Macgregor.

00:24:26.087 --> 00:24:28.270
And you were never harassed by Mr. Hunt.

00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:29.222
- That's a lie.

00:24:29.222 --> 00:24:30.970
- Look, I can tell you that
I've got the canceled checks

00:24:30.970 --> 00:24:33.421
or the wire transfer
- this doesn't make any sense.

00:24:33.421 --> 00:24:35.930
Okay, here's why this
doesn't make any sense.

00:24:35.930 --> 00:24:38.640
They're hinting that the other side

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:42.900
hired a witness to
testify that the defendant

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:45.900
sexually harassed her in
some sort of quid pro quo

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:49.130
sexual harassment, and then
she was going to basically

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:52.370
purger herself and admit
to not being truthful

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:55.850
about a prior crime when she was 17.

00:24:55.850 --> 00:24:57.930
That doesn't make any
sense because her testimony

00:24:57.930 --> 00:24:59.290
is still on the record.

00:24:59.290 --> 00:25:02.960
She testified that he
sexually harassed her.

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:04.240
So they hired someone

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:06.510
to provide evidence for the other side.

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:09.040
Now true, they might have
to deal with the fallout

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:12.520
of her being shown to be not
the most trustworthy person

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:15.390
but that testimony is still on the record.

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.940
They hired someone, they paid someone,

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:21.470
to give horrible testimony
about the defendant.

00:25:21.470 --> 00:25:25.030
They're not even witness
tampering right, it's crazy.

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:27.853
- Wire transfer from your
account to Joanna Webster's.

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:32.323
Phone records with Joanna
Webster prior to her testimony.

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:36.410
And an affidavit stating
you paid Miss. Webster

00:25:36.410 --> 00:25:37.643
to falsely testify.

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:41.660
- But, again, she falsely testified

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:44.150
against the people that hired her.

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:48.070
That's not even the way
you'd wanna witness tamper.

00:25:48.070 --> 00:25:51.136
They paid someone to give
testimony against themselves.

00:25:51.136 --> 00:25:53.720
It's totally insane.

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:54.553
- What do you want?

00:25:54.553 --> 00:25:56.200
- An admission of guilt

00:25:56.200 --> 00:25:58.020
and a guarantee that
you'll obtain treatment

00:25:58.020 --> 00:25:59.430
before working again.

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:02.600
- Again, there's nothing
for him to admit guilt for,

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:05.420
except maybe witness
tampering if that is an actual

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:07.070
criminal crime.

00:26:07.070 --> 00:26:09.370
This is a civil case
for sexual harassment,

00:26:09.370 --> 00:26:11.170
quid pro quo sexual harassment

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:14.320
and he needs to pay the plaintiff money

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:17.910
and there might be an
admission of liability

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:19.760
but the most important thing

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:20.930
is that he's gonna compensate her

00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:21.970
for the wrongs that she's done.

00:26:21.970 --> 00:26:24.920
That's how the civil system works.

00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:28.540
You put people in jail
to punish them for crimes

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:30.850
against really the state,
and the people in general.

00:26:30.850 --> 00:26:32.630
You are civilly liable for things

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:33.800
that you've done to an individual.

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:35.640
In this case, sexual harassment.

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:36.843
- [Judge] All rise.

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:40.800
- That was Suits, episode one.

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:42.530
A lot of things going on there,

00:26:42.530 --> 00:26:43.500
a lot of moving pieces.

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:45.720
So how does it stack up
against the real world?

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:47.170
What grade does it get?

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:48.490
Well, let's see.

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:50.870
In terms of the recruiting
process in law school

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:52.730
